  - [38;5;153mset()[39m called with a non-string or empty userId — now specified to write nothing, notify nobody, return [38;5;153mfalse[39m.
  - What [38;5;153monChange[39m subscribers receive — now the userId after [38;5;153mset[39m, [38;5;153mnull[39m after [38;5;153mclear[39m.
  - Whether an unchanged [38;5;153mset()[39m still notifies — now yes, unconditionally.
  - Whether [38;5;153mget()[39m clearing an expired record notifies — now explicitly [1mno[0m, with the reentrancy reasoning recorded.

  Test cases covering all four were added to the testing section.

  Also created [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m with [38;5;153mdocs/superpowers[39m and [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers[39m, per your global instruction that spec and planning
  docs stay uncommitted. The repo had no [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m. Nothing is committed.

  ---

  Spec written to [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-30-user-identity-layer-design.md[39m (not committed). Please review it and
  let me know if you want to make any changes before we start writing out the implementation plan.

  Two things in it worth your attention specifically, since they're where I made a judgment call:

  1. [1mThe stale-identity window[0m is documented as a known limitation rather than solved — between the session cookie
     expiring and the 24h TTL lapsing, [38;5;153mget()[39m returns a userId for a dead session, and with no backend there's no way to
     detect it. The resolution is attached (401 → [38;5;153mclear()[39m) but it can't be built now.
  2. [1mThe spec says in its summary that no authentication exists.[0m I put it there rather than in a footnote deliberately.
     If this spec gets handed to someone who skims, that's the line that stops them shipping a fake identity as real
     auth.

